O = {
1:"Go deeper: notice how Genesis 1 and 2 answer different questions. Chapter 1 shows God's power and order, and chapter 2 shows His personal care for the man and the woman He made. The fall in chapter 3 is a choice to doubt what God said, and every sin since has begun in the same way. Yet God does not abandon Adam and Eve. He clothes them and gives a promise. As you read, ask what the story teaches about who God is, who we are, and why the world needs a Savior.",
2:"Go deeper: Abraham is not remembered for being perfect. He is remembered for believing God. Genesis 15:6 says he believed in the LORD, and it was counted to him for righteousness, and the New Testament returns to that verse to teach that faith has always been the way to please God. The story of Joseph shows the same trust over a long road: betrayal, prison and promotion did not defeat God's plan. As you study, trace how one promise is handed down through one family, because that thread leads to Christ.",
3:"Go deeper: the Passover and the Exodus are the Old Testament's great picture of salvation. The blood on the doorposts, the crossing of the sea and the giving of the law show a God who rescues first and then instructs. Israel was brought out of Egypt before the commandments were given at Sinai, so obedience is the response of a redeemed people, not the price of rescue. Ask yourself what the Passover lamb teaches, and keep the question in mind until you reach John 1.",
4:"Go deeper: Leviticus can feel distant, but it teaches that a holy God can dwell among sinful people only through atonement. The tabernacle, the priests and the sacrifices all say that sin is serious and that God provides a way to be near Him. Numbers 6 gives the priestly blessing that Israel heard for generations. Deuteronomy 6 gives the heart of the law, to love the LORD with all the heart, soul and might, and to teach it to the children. Read each book as God showing His people how to live with Him.",
5:"Go deeper: Joshua opens with a command repeated more than once, Be strong and of a good courage, and the courage rests on God's presence and His word, not on Joshua's ability. The fall of Jericho shows that victory came by obedience to God's instructions. Joshua 24 ends with a choice that every generation must make. Notice that Joshua leads by example before he asks the people to follow, saying that he and his house will serve the LORD.",
6:"Go deeper: the Book of Judges repeats a sad cycle: the people sin, they are oppressed, they cry out, and God raises up a deliverer. The last verse says that every man did that which was right in his own eyes, which explains why the nation was in trouble. Ruth shines against that dark background. A foreign widow shows loyalty and faith, and Boaz acts as a kinsman redeemer. Ruth becomes part of the line of David, and so of the line of Christ.",
7:"Go deeper: Israel asked for a king, and Samuel, Saul, David and Solomon show both the promise and the problem of earthly kingship. Saul's pride led to his rejection. David was a man after God's own heart, though he was not without sin, and God made him a covenant promise in 2 Samuel 7 that his throne would be established forever. Solomon asked for wisdom and built the temple. Watch for how the covenant with David points beyond David to a greater king.",
8:"Go deeper: after Solomon the kingdom divided, and both the north and the south drifted into idolatry despite many prophets who warned them. The exile was judgment, but it was never the end of God's purpose. A remnant returned. Ezra taught the law, Nehemiah rebuilt the wall, and in Esther God's hand is seen working through events without His name being spoken. Ask what these books teach about God's faithfulness when His people are faithless.",
9:"Go deeper: Job is a book about suffering and trust. Job loses nearly everything, and his friends insist that his pain must be punishment for sin. Job does not understand, but he does not curse God, and he cries out for a redeemer in chapter 19. In the end God speaks, and Job learns that God's wisdom is greater than his questions. The book does not explain every reason for pain. It teaches us to keep trusting a God who is good and who is in control.",
10:"Go deeper: the Psalms teach us how to talk to God. Some are songs of praise, some are laments full of honest pain, and some are prayers of trust. Hebrew poetry uses parallelism, where a second line repeats, contrasts or deepens the first. Psalm 1 contrasts two ways of living, Psalm 19 says that creation and the word of God both speak, Psalm 23 pictures God as shepherd, and Psalm 100 invites everyone to thank Him. Read them aloud and make them part of your own prayers.",
11:"Go deeper: Proverbs teaches wisdom for everyday life, and its starting point is stated in chapter 1: the fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge. Wisdom shows up in the way we speak, work, handle money and choose friends. Proverbs speaks in general principles, not in unbreakable promises, so it must be read with the whole of Scripture. Pick one proverb from each chapter you read and ask how it should change what you do this week.",
12:"Go deeper: Ecclesiastes asks what life means when everything is seen only under the sun. The Preacher finds that work, pleasure and wealth cannot satisfy by themselves, and he calls it vanity. His conclusion in chapter 12 is clear: fear God and keep His commandments. Song of Solomon celebrates faithful married love as God's gift, and many readers also see in it a picture of the love between God and His people. Together these books teach us to receive life as a gift from God.",
13:"Go deeper: Isaiah's call begins with a vision of the holiness of God, and only then does he hear the question, Whom shall I send? Seeing God's holiness leads to confession, cleansing and service. The book moves from judgment to comfort, and chapters 7, 9, 53 and 61 look ahead to the Messiah. The New Testament quotes Isaiah often. As you read, list every promise that Christians believe was fulfilled in Jesus.",
14:"Go deeper: Jeremiah was called while young and served for decades with little visible success. He preached repentance, warned of Babylon, and wept over the people's sin. Yet his book carries hope: in chapter 29 God tells the exiles to settle, to seek the peace of the city, and to trust that He has plans for them. In chapter 31 He promises a new covenant written on the heart. Lamentations teaches that you can grieve honestly and still say that God's mercies are new every morning.",
15:"Go deeper: Ezekiel served among the exiles in Babylon, and he saw the glory of God there, which taught the people that God was not confined to the temple in Jerusalem. He was a watchman who was responsible to warn. His book also carries promises: God Himself will shepherd His flock, give His people a new heart and a new spirit, and breathe life into the dry bones. Ask what each of these promises shows about God's power to restore what looks dead.",
16:"Go deeper: Daniel and his friends lived faithfully in a foreign empire. They refused to defile themselves, they refused to bow to the image, and Daniel kept praying even when it was forbidden. Each time God was with them in the trial. The visions in the second half of the book show that God rules over the kingdoms of the world and that His kingdom will last forever. Ask what it costs to live with integrity where the culture does not share your faith.",
17:"Go deeper: Hosea shows God's faithful love through a prophet who is told to love an unfaithful wife, a picture of how God loves a straying people. Joel calls for a return to God with the whole heart and looks ahead to the pouring out of the Spirit, which Peter later explains in Acts 2. Amos cries out for justice and righteousness and warns that worship without a just life is empty. Each prophet calls the people back to the covenant.",
18:"Go deeper: Obadiah warns that pride brings a fall. Jonah is a story about the mercy of God. The prophet runs away, God sends a storm and a great fish, and in the end Nineveh repents. The book ends with a question about God's compassion, and it asks us to look at our own hearts. Micah 6:8 sums up what the LORD requires: to do justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with God. Micah 5:2 names Bethlehem as the birthplace of the ruler to come.",
19:"Go deeper: these three prophets teach us how to trust God when evil seems to win. Nahum shows that God is good and that He will judge Nineveh. Habakkuk asks honest questions, how long and why, and God answers that the just shall live by his faith. The book ends with a song of joy that does not depend on circumstances. Zephaniah promises that the LORD in the midst of His people will rejoice over them with singing. Ask how you can bring hard questions to God and still worship.",
20:"Go deeper: after returning from exile, the people left the temple unfinished while they built their own houses. Haggai challenged their priorities, and Zechariah reminded them that the work would be done not by might nor by power, but by the Spirit of the LORD. Zechariah 9:9 pictures a king coming humble and riding on a donkey. Malachi closes the Old Testament by promising a messenger who will prepare the way. The prophets leave us waiting, which prepares us for the New Testament.",
21:"Go deeper: the Bible is one story, and the covenants are its thread. God promised Abraham a blessing for all nations, promised David an everlasting throne, and promised through Jeremiah a new covenant written on the heart. In Luke 24 the risen Jesus shows His disciples that all the Scriptures speak of Him. When you read the Old Testament, look for how each covenant promise comes closer to its fulfillment, and then see how the New Testament names that fulfillment.",
22:"Go deeper: the prophecies give details written long before the events. Isaiah 7 speaks of a virgin and a son named Immanuel. Micah 5 names Bethlehem. Isaiah 53 describes a servant who bears our griefs and is wounded for our transgressions. Psalm 22 describes a death by suffering, and Psalm 110 speaks of one who is both king and priest. Read them slowly and record which details the Gospels report. Then notice that the fulfillment was not forced, since many of these writings were separated from Christ by centuries.",
23:"Go deeper: sacrifice, priesthood and the coming prophet all point to Christ. Isaac on Mount Moriah is spared when God provides a ram, which teaches that God provides the offering. Leviticus 17:11 says the life of the flesh is in the blood and that blood makes atonement. The high priest entered on the Day of Atonement once a year. John 1:29 declares Jesus the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world. Ask how the Old Testament system prepared people to understand what Jesus did.",
24:"Go deeper: the four hundred years between Malachi and Matthew are sometimes called the silent years, but God was preparing the world. Many people spoke one common language, Roman roads made travel easier, and many hearts were waiting. Galatians 4:4 says that when the fulness of the time was come, God sent forth His Son. Isaiah 40 and Malachi 3 promise a messenger who prepares the way, and the New Testament says John the Baptist was that messenger.",
}
N = {
1:"Go deeper: each Gospel presents Jesus to a different audience. Matthew writes especially to show how Jesus fulfills the Old Testament, Mark presents Him as the active servant, Luke shows His compassion for all people, and John declares that the Word was God and was made flesh. The birth narratives show that God kept His promise in a humble way, with a manger and shepherds. Ask why God chose to come as a child, and what that tells you about His love.",
2:"Go deeper: Jesus was baptized, and then He was tempted. Each time the devil tempted Him, Jesus answered with the written word, saying, It is written. That is a model for us. The Sermon on the Mount describes the character of the kingdom, from the Beatitudes to prayer, giving, forgiving and trusting the Father. Jesus calls disciples to a righteousness that begins in the heart. Choose one teaching from chapters 5 and 6 and plan how to practice it this week.",
3:"Go deeper: the miracles of Jesus show His authority over storms, demons, sickness and even death. They are signs that point to who He is, and each one is also an act of compassion. His parables use ordinary things, like seeds, sheep and sons, to teach about the kingdom. In Luke 15 the lost sheep, coin and son show a God who searches and rejoices. The raising of Lazarus in John 11 points ahead to Jesus's own resurrection, as He says, I am the resurrection, and the life.",
4:"Go deeper: the Gospels give much space to the last week of Jesus's life because the cross and the resurrection are the center of the gospel. He was crucified in the place of sinners, and He cried out that it was finished. John 3:16 gives the reason, that God so loved the world. On the third day the tomb was empty and Jesus appeared to His disciples. In Matthew 28 He commissions them to go and teach all nations. The resurrection proves that He is who He said He was.",
5:"Go deeper: Acts begins where Luke ends. Jesus promised that His disciples would receive power after the Holy Ghost came upon them, and that they would be witnesses in Jerusalem, Judea, Samaria and to the uttermost part of the earth. On the day of Pentecost the Spirit was poured out, and Peter preached that the people should repent and be baptized in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of sins, and receive the gift of the Holy Ghost. About three thousand were added that day.",
6:"Go deeper: the early church grew through bold witness. In Acts 3 a lame man is healed in the name of Jesus, and Peter explains that the power came from Him. When the leaders threatened them, the apostles answered that there is no other name under heaven given among men whereby we must be saved. Stephen became the first martyr, and Saul stood by approving. Then Saul met Jesus on the road to Damascus and was changed. The persecutor became a preacher, which shows what God's grace can do.",
7:"Go deeper: Acts 10 is a turning point. God shows Peter in a vision that he should not call any person common or unclean, and Cornelius, a Gentile, receives the Holy Ghost as the Jews had. The church at Antioch sends out Barnabas and Saul on the first missionary journey. In Acts 15 the council in Jerusalem agrees that Gentiles are saved by grace, not by keeping the law of Moses. Ask what this teaches about the church welcoming all kinds of people.",
8:"Go deeper: Paul's journeys carried the gospel across the Roman world. In Philippi a jailer and his household believe after an earthquake. In Berea the people searched the Scriptures daily to see whether Paul's message was true, which is a good example for every student. At Athens Paul preached to philosophers. In Acts 20 he says farewell to the Ephesian elders, and Acts ends with Paul preaching in Rome, unhindered. Notice that he kept preaching through prison and hardship.",
9:"Go deeper: Romans lays out the gospel in order. All have sinned and come short of the glory of God. We are justified freely by His grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus. Chapter 5 says that while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us. Chapter 6 asks whether grace gives us license to sin, and answers no, because we have been raised to walk in newness of life. Read slowly and write down each step of Paul's argument.",
10:"Go deeper: Romans 8 begins with no condemnation to those who are in Christ Jesus and ends with the assurance that nothing can separate us from the love of God. The Spirit leads, helps us pray and testifies that we are children of God. Romans 10 says that whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord shall be saved, and asks how they will hear without a preacher. Romans 12 calls us to present our bodies as a living sacrifice and to be transformed by the renewing of our minds.",
11:"Go deeper: the Corinthian church had gifts, but it also had divisions. In 1 Corinthians 12 Paul teaches that the church is one body with many members, and each one is needed. Chapter 13 shows that without love, even great gifts count for nothing. Chapter 15 defends the resurrection as the foundation of the faith. In 2 Corinthians 5 he says that if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature, and in chapter 12 he learns that God's grace is sufficient in weakness.",
12:"Go deeper: Galatians was written to churches that were being told they must add the works of the law to faith in Christ. Paul answers that a man is justified by the faith of Jesus Christ, not by the works of the law, and that Christ has made us free. He warns against using that freedom as an occasion for the flesh. Instead we are to serve one another in love and walk in the Spirit. The fruit of the Spirit in chapter 5 describes what a Spirit led life looks like.",
13:"Go deeper: Ephesians moves from what God has done to how we should live. We are saved by grace through faith, it is the gift of God, and we are His workmanship created unto good works. Chapter 4 calls the church to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace. Chapter 6 describes the armor of God, from the belt of truth to the sword of the Spirit, which is the word of God. Choose one piece of armor and describe how you will use it this week.",
14:"Go deeper: Paul wrote Philippians from prison, yet the letter is full of joy. In chapter 2 he points to the humility of Christ, who took the form of a servant, and in chapter 4 he says that he has learned to be content in every state and that he can do all things through Christ who strengthens him. Colossians declares that Christ has the preeminence in all things. Chapter 3 teaches us to set our affections on things above and to put on love.",
15:"Go deeper: Paul wrote to the Thessalonians to encourage a young church that faced pressure. He comforts them about those who have died in Christ and teaches that the Lord Himself shall descend from heaven, and so shall we ever be with the Lord. He also tells them to comfort one another with these words. Chapter 5 urges them to be watchful and to rejoice evermore and pray without ceasing. In 2 Thessalonians 3 he reminds them to keep working faithfully while they wait.",
16:"Go deeper: Paul's letters to Timothy and Titus are written to young pastors. He urges Timothy to be an example of the believers in word, in conversation, in charity, in spirit, in faith and in purity. In 2 Timothy 3:16 he teaches that all scripture is given by inspiration of God and is profitable for doctrine, reproof, correction and instruction in righteousness. Titus 2 teaches sound doctrine and good works. Philemon is a short letter that shows how the gospel changes a relationship, as Paul asks Philemon to receive his runaway servant Onesimus as a brother.",
17:"Go deeper: Hebrews shows that Jesus is better than the prophets, the angels, Moses and the Levitical priests. He is our great high priest who can be touched with the feeling of our infirmities, so we may come boldly to the throne of grace. Chapter 11 recounts the heroes of faith, who obtained a good report through faith. Chapter 12 tells us to lay aside every weight and the sin that so easily besets us, and to run with patience the race that is set before us, looking unto Jesus.",
18:"Go deeper: James is practical. He says to count it all joy when you fall into diverse temptations, because the trying of your faith worketh patience. He promises that anyone who lacks wisdom may ask of God. He warns that faith without works is dead, which means that real faith shows itself in action. He also warns about the power of the tongue and urges humility. Choose one command from James and write how you will obey it this week.",
19:"Go deeper: Peter writes to believers who suffer. He opens with praise that God has begotten us again unto a lively hope by the resurrection of Jesus Christ. He calls the church a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, a holy nation. He tells elders to feed the flock and tells all to cast their care upon God, because He cares for us. In 2 Peter 3 he reminds us that the Lord is not slack concerning His promise, and he urges us to grow in grace and in the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ.",
20:"Go deeper: John writes about light and love. If we walk in the light, as He is in the light, we have fellowship one with another, and the blood of Jesus Christ cleanseth us from all sin. If we confess our sins, He is faithful and just to forgive us. Chapter 4 says that God is love, and that we love because He first loved us. Chapter 5 says that whatsoever is born of God overcometh the world. Jude urges believers to earnestly contend for the faith which was once delivered unto the saints.",
21:"Go deeper: Revelation begins with John on the isle of Patmos, in exile, seeing the risen Christ among the lampstands. The letters to the seven churches in chapters 2 and 3 show how Jesus knows His church. He commends faithfulness, corrects error, and calls each church to repent or hold fast. Each letter ends with a promise to him that overcometh. As you read, ask what Christ praises, what He corrects, and which letter speaks most directly to churches today.",
22:"Go deeper: Revelation 4 and 5 give a view of heaven. The throne is at the center, and all heaven worships the One who sits on it. A scroll that no one else can open is opened by the Lamb that was slain, and the worship that follows declares that He is worthy. Chapter 7 shows a great multitude from every nation, kindred, people and tongue standing before the throne. Even in a book about judgment, worship is at the center.",
23:"Go deeper: Revelation 12 shows that believers overcame the accuser by the blood of the Lamb and by the word of their testimony. In chapter 19 Christ comes as Faithful and True, with a name written, King of kings and Lord of lords. Chapter 20 describes the final judgment, when the dead are judged and evil is put away. The message to believers is not fear but confidence, because the Lord has won the victory. Faithfulness now is shaped by the certainty of that end.",
24:"Go deeper: the Bible ends with a promise. Revelation 21 describes a new heaven and a new earth, where God dwells with His people and wipes away all tears from their eyes. There is no more death, sorrow or crying. The One on the throne says, Behold, I make all things new. Revelation 22 offers the water of life freely and ends with an invitation: whosoever will, let him take the water of life freely. Close the course by asking how this hope shapes the way you live today.",
}
